carries more than these two\0A    // buffers, so it is released by the function that knows about all\0A    // of it rather than half-released here.\0A    if (plum_process_slots[handle].child != NULL) {\0A        process_child_free(handle);\0A        return;\0A    }\0A    free(plum_process_slots[handle].out_data);\0A    free(plum_process_slots[handle].err_data);\0A    plum_process_slots[handle].in_use = 0;\0A}\0A\0A// --- Asynchronous children (issue #7) ---\0A//\0A// `process_run` blocks, which is the right shape for a compiler\0A// shelling out to `clang` and the wrong one for an application that\0A// wants to stay responsive while something runs. These add the other\0A// shape: start it, look in on it, stop it.\0A//\0A// The temp-file capture is what makes this cheap. A pipe-based child\0A// would need somebody draining it the whole time the child runs -- the\0A// deadlock this file's header describes -- so `poll` could not be a\0A// question you ask occasionally. A file has no fixed buffer, so nothing\0A// has to be read until the child is gone, and `poll` really is just\0A// `waitpid(WNOHANG)`.\0A//\0A// **The status is collected exactly once.** The transition from running\0A// to finished reads both files, deletes them, and fills the same\0A// `out_data`/`err_data` the blocking path fills -- so\0A// `process_exit_code`, `process_stdout_data` and `process_stderr_data`\0A// work unchanged afterwards, and work repeatedly. There is deliberately\0A// no way to read a child's output before it exits: that would mean\0A// defining what a partial read of a file somebody is still writing\0A// means, and no caller has asked for it.\0A\0Astatic void plum_child_collect(long long handle, long long code, int capture) {\0A    PlumProcessSlot *slot = &plum_process_slots[handle];\0A    PlumChildState *c = slot->child;\0A    if (c == NULL || c->finished) return;\0A    slot->exit_code = code;\0A#if defined(_WIN32)\0A    // Closed only now, so everything the child wrote is flushed 